The testcase is not fully accurate. `Old_arrList_IndexOf_x` and `Old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x` are from the old array list before the test case, while `New_arrList_IndexOf_x` and `New_arrList_LastIndexOf_x` are from the new list after each invocation of the method. If there is no change in the `x` in the new list for the assertion `New_arrList_LastIndexOf_x >= Old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x` to hold, these would be equal in values. But the assertion `New_arrList_IndexOf_x >= Old_arrList_IndexOf_x` still holds true. This is because `IndexOf()` and `LastIndexOf()` return either the index of the first or last occurrence of the specified item in the list, or `-1` if the list does not contain the item. But the index of the first or last occurrence of `x` in the old list is surely less than or equal to the index in the new list for it to be valid. This is not 100% accurate but works better for most common inputs and test cases. We should consider how `IndexOf()` and `LastIndexOf()` behave in corner cases like removing an element from some position other than the end. The implementation of these functions may also change based on the language or version of the framework.--------------------
